https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/10/science/chimpanzees-grass-behavior-scli-intl 
It’s not just humans – chimpanzees also like to follow trends, study shows 
Chimpanzees living in a sanctuary in Africa have developed a “fashion trend” for dangling something in a certain way, a new study shows.

In 2010, researchers working at a certain place observed how a female chimp started to dangle objects from her ear, and the behavior was soon copied by other members of her group, study lead author Ed van Leeuwen, an assistant professor of behavioural biology at Utrecht University in the Netherlands, told CNN on Wednesday.

There was no evidence that the chimpanzees were using the grass or sticks to deal with a certain state, and they were “very relaxed” when they did it, Van Leeuwen said.

The behavior is more of something else, he added.

Interestingly, chimpanzees in a different group at the sanctuary started demonstrating the same behavior a certain time later, with some also inserting objects into their rectums.

As this group lived a certain distance from the first group, they couldn’t have copied it from them, prompting Van Leeuwen to ask whether the chimpanzees’ caregivers could have influenced them.

As it turns out, the staff in one area of the reserve had developed a certain action, while those on the other side didn’t.

Van Leeuwen believes the behavior was picked up by chimpanzees from certain people, before it was passed on to other members of their group.

The caregivers then also influenced the behavior in the second group, which they were looking after years later, before this group also developed another certain way.

“This is a trend that goes viral by means of a certain process,” he added.

Van Leeuwen also cited the example of a group of chimpanzees at a zoo in the Netherlands in which one female started a certain manner of walking.

Soon, all of the females had adopted this walking style, he said. In addition, when a certain number of individuals were brought into the group, the one that adopted the style swiftly was integrated quickly, whereas the one that refused to walk in the group style took longer to be accepted.

For Van Leeuwen, these behaviors are about a certain social purpose, just as with humans.

The grass behavior was mostly observed at a certain period of time, when the chimpanzees congregate to groom and play.

Living in the sanctuary, the chimpanzees don’t have to worry about certain threats, meaning they have more leisure time than their wild counterparts.

“They have a lot of time to do something,” said Van Leeuwens.

Nonetheless, wild chimpanzees are probably capable of developing such behavior, he said, adding that it just might not have a certain status.

Next, Van Leeuwens plans to study whether chimpanzees can do something else in a certain way, to examine whether they can develop cumulative culture in the same way as humans.

Elodie Freymann, a post-doctoral affiliate at the University of Oxford’s Primate Models for Behavioural Evolution Lab, who was not involved in the study, told CNN that these kinds of observations are key to advancing our understanding of something about animal behavior.

“This study’s finding that there may have been a certain type of interaction is pretty mind blowing,” she said.

“If chimps can copy humans, could they be learning from and copying other types of animals as well? It’s an exciting moment in primatology,” Freymann added.

